  "annotation": {
    "version": 1,
    "task": "Record reviews beside the glosses, so someone reviewing a teammate's branch can see whether each commit's gloss was checked, what the check found, and whether it still applies",
    "summary": "A new notes ref holds review records, one JSON line each, so everyone's records combine without conflicts. Each record keeps what the reviewer saw (a fingerprint of each changed line and of each gloss group) and its verdict. From those, show, the HTML view and `git gloss reviews` say per stage whether the commit as it is now was reviewed, what was concluded, and whether the code or gloss changed since. Records follow adopt and carry, travel with sync and pushes, and are copied through rebases. git-gud records through `reviews --stamp` and `--record`.",
    "groups": [
      {
        "id": "reviews-core",
        "intent": "Review records: storage, content fingerprints, per-stage status and verdict",
        "necessity": "required",
        "serves": "task",
        "removal_impact": "there would be nothing to record reviews in or to judge whether a review still applies",
        "evidence": "test/reviews.test.ts: status follows gloss, risk and code changes, repeated lines, squashes; verdict rules incl. a verdict-less later review and a quoted verdict; 8 concurrent records all kept (the test fails without the retry; a check-and-retry alone still lost one in three runs); a binary file added after review shows as new code; a forged record with an unknown verdict is ignored",
        "hunks": [
          "h36"
        ],
        "notes": "Line keys hash file plus line text, one key per occurrence, so a second copy of a reviewed line isn't 'seen'; coverage adds up counts across distinct stamps (a squash's source reviews), counting the same stamp once. Gloss keys cover each group (its meaning, serves targets included, without id, hunk refs or the notes carrying adds) and each risk and rejected alternative, keyed one by one so combined glosses still match; task and summary are left out because carrying joins them. A stage's verdict takes, for each part, the latest review with a verdict that saw it (a review without a VERDICT line doesn't override), and an earlier review with issues stays visible. The VERDICT line must be one of the report's last three lines, at line start. Records are validated when read, so a malformed one from another machine is skipped. Writes are a compare-and-swap on the notes ref (built under a private ref, moved only if unchanged), because concurrent recorders lost reviews: 12 parallel records kept 10, and a check-and-retry still lost one in three runs. Per-stage wording: improve says 'suggestions', bugs 'defects found'; a review taken before the commit had a gloss says so. Most of this came from git-gud's own reviews of the change. A change with no lines (a binary file, a mode change) is keyed by file and content hash, so adding one shows as new code. Records with an unknown verdict, model or mode are skipped; a note joined by a rebase is read once per line. --record checks every stamped commit exists before writing anything. The VERDICT line must be a line of its own. Temporary refs for the compare-and-swap are named outside the gloss* glob."
      },
